# Plan Domain

Assembles, fingerprints, verifies, and persists plans. May depend on `@wings/math-engine` and on the `PlanStore` interface. MUST NOT depend on React, Netlify, or any concrete database client.

Division of labor: math-engine computes, plan-domain assembles and verifies, the UI renders. Never move lifecycle logic into math-engine — it is stateful and would break purity.

## Immutability

An ADOPTED plan version cannot be altered. This is enforced in two places and both must stay:

1. Reject an incoming draft that targets an adopted plan.
2. Reject a write whose ID matches an already-adopted stored record.

The second check exists because the first alone allows a caller to reuse an adopted ID and overwrite it. Do not remove it as redundant.

A reforecast is a new child plan version with a new version ID and a `parentPlanVersionId`. It never mutates its parent.

## Canonical fingerprints

`hash.ts` produces a fingerprint stable across object-key ordering. If you touch it, the existing stability tests must still pass. Any change to canonicalization invalidates every stored fingerprint — treat it as a breaking change and say so.

## Adoption gates

Adoption verifies four things. Removing any one of them is a correctness regression:

1. Current canonical input fingerprint matches the saved draft fingerprint.
2. It matches the fingerprint on every material calculation snapshot.
3. It matches `expectedInputHash`, the fingerprint of the plan the manager was reviewing when they clicked Adopt. Checks 1 and 2 verify data consistency; only this one verifies the manager saw the version being adopted.
4. Every material feasibility gap carries a current, non-stale acknowledgment.

Plus: every section marked `requiredForAdoption` is complete. Section requirements are evaluated against the enabled program objective set, so a campaign that has not enabled an optional objective is not blocked on its fields.

Adoption never silently recomputes. It refuses and reports.

## Feasibility acknowledgments

An acknowledgment stores its own snapshot — constraint type, strategic metric and value, operational metric and value, gap at acceptance, manager reason, actor, timestamp — plus a `gapFingerprint`. It does not merely reference a gap by ID, because the record must stay intelligible months later after assumptions change.

An acknowledgment authorizes exactly the gap fingerprint it was created against. If the gap changes, it is stale and adoption fails with `FEASIBILITY_ACK_STALE`.

Constraint types: `CAPACITY`, `COST`, `REACHABILITY`, `ALLOCATION`, `OTHER`.

## Errors

`errors.ts` exports typed errors carrying structured context so the UI can route without its own mapping. Never replace a typed error with a string. When adding a failure mode, add a code and the context needed to route to the responsible screen.

## Plan Builder

`builder.ts` is the ONLY producer of `PlanVersionRecord`, input fingerprints, and calculation snapshots. No screen and no other module may serialize a plan.

The builder may serialize incomplete drafts — that is legitimate intermediate state. It returns per-section status with exact missing keys. Incomplete drafts save; they do not adopt.
